### 🔹 Line 3 — The WeakMap Counter

```ts
const stepCounters = new WeakMap<TestInfo, number>();
```

**What is a WeakMap?**  
A `Map` that uses objects as keys and auto-deletes entries when the object is garbage collected.

```
WeakMap<TestInfo, number>
         ↑           ↑
      KEY: the    VALUE: step
      TestInfo    counter (0,1,2...)
      object
```

**Why not a plain `number` variable?**  
Because tests run in **parallel**. If you used a single `let counter = 0`, two tests running at the same time would share and corrupt the same counter.

```ts
// ❌ WRONG — shared across all tests
let counter = 0;

// ✅ CORRECT — each TestInfo is unique per test
const stepCounters = new WeakMap<TestInfo, number>();
```

Each test gets its own `TestInfo` object → its own counter starting from `0`.

**Why WeakMap and not Map?**  
When a test finishes, its `TestInfo` object gets garbage collected → WeakMap automatically removes the entry too. No memory leak. Plain `Map` would hold the reference forever.

